    "message": {
      "content": "# Distributed Tensor and Expert Parallel Inference: A Technical Note\n\n## 1. Overview and Motivation\n\nModern large models exceed the memory and compute capacity of a single accelerator. Two complementary parallelism strategies address this during inference:\n\n- **Tensor Parallelism (TP)**: Splits individual weight matrices (and thus individual layers' compute) across devices. Every device holds a shard of every layer and participates in every forward pass.\n- **Expert Parallelism (EP)**: Splits the *experts* of a Mixture-of-Experts (MoE) layer across devices. Each device holds a subset of experts in full; tokens are routed to whichever device owns the relevant expert.\n\nIn practice, serving stacks combine them: TP for dense attention/MLP blocks (which are memory-bound and benefit from splitting large matrices), EP for MoE layers (where splitting experts avoids the all-reduce-heavy alternative of TP-sharding every expert). A typical configuration is `TP=2/4` within a node (NVLink domain) and `EP=8/16/32` across nodes, since EP's communication pattern tolerates inter-node bandwidth better than TP does.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Tensor Parallel Inference\n\n### 2.1 Sharding scheme\n\nConsider a transformer layer. The two dominant matrix multiplies are attention projections and MLP projections.\n\n**MLP block (Megatron-style):**\n\n```\nY = GeLU(X\u00b7W1) \u00b7 W2        # W1: [d, 4d], W2: [4d, d]\n```\n\n- `W1` is **column-parallel**: split along output dimension, `W1 = [W1^(0); W1^(1); ...]`. Each rank computes `Y_i = GeLU(X\u00b7W1^(i))` \u2014 a `[B, S, 4d/P]` activation. The GeLU is applied locally, which is valid because GeLU is",
      "reasoning": "",
      "role": "assistant"
    },
